  "worked_solution": "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C+6I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C+6I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ has dimension $6.$ This means that the solutions to $(C+6I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $6.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $6,$ our basis can contain only $4$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C+6I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(C+6I)^{3}=6-2=4\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6.$",
